Task 1:
///Lab: Exploiting an API endpoint using documentation///

Open the site in burp, log in and change your email to anything. Check http history. There will be a PATCH /api/user/wiener request. Send it to request. Modify the request and delete the wiener part from the url.
You will get an 400 bad request. Now delete user part too so the url will be /api. And you will get 302 found. So check this in browser, and you will see this:
GET	/user/[username: String]	{ }	200 OK, User
DELETE	/user/[username: String]	{ }	200 OK, Result
PATCH	/user/[username: String]	{"email": String}	200 OK, User

all you have to do not is to modify the request method to DELETE and the url to /api/user/carlos, send the request, and thats all.

Task 2:
///Lab: Exploiting server-side parameter pollution in a query string///

Open the site in burp. To solve the lab we have to log in as administrtator. On the My account page there is a forgot password option, click on it, and as username type administrator in.
Check http history and send the POST /forgot-password request to repeater. Lets play with this request. Modify the administrator name to administratorxy-->you will get invalid username.
So add a new paramterer like administrator&1=1---> Parameter is not supported (always use url encode). Now change it to administrator#--->Field not specified...okay we have a clue. Lets specify the field parameter:
administrator&field=1: --> {"type":"ClientError","code":400,"error":"Invalid field."
Send this request to intruder. Add a postition to the '1' parameter, and in payload configuration load from a list called: Server-side variable names. Start the attack, and check all the 200 responses.
I got 2: email and username. if i type field=username# ---> {"result":"administrator","type":"username"}
Now from the http history check this request:
GET /static/js/forgotPassword.js
In this javascript there is a line:
  {
        window.location.href = `/forgot-password?reset_token=${resetToken}`;
    }
Lets try this reset_token as field: {"result":"fn9zeqlq2r2mchyhmft4gtb564reb8mn","type":"reset_token"}
So we have a url and a reset token...together:
https://0a5a00530463104c81217a5c00ab003b.web-security-academy.net/forgot-password?reset_token=fn9zeqlq2r2mchyhmft4gtb564reb8mn
Set a new password, log in, and delete carlos.

Task 3:
///Lab: Finding and exploiting an unused API endpoint///

Open the site in burp, log in as wiener/peter, add the leather jacket to you cart, try to purchase it, then remove from the cart, and check httpy history. Check the /resources/js/api/productPrice.js file.
There is this line:
   fetch(`//${url.host}/api/products/${encodeURIComponent(productId)}/price`)
So this is where the site fetching the products price. Check this request: GET /api/products/1/price HTTP/2 and send it to repeater.
Change the request method to OPTIONS. As you can see its not allowed, but PATCH is allowed, so change it to PATCH:
{"type":"ClientError","code":400,"error":"Only 'application/json' Content-Type is supported"}
Add a new header: Content-Type: application/json and an empty json object: {}

{"type":"ClientError","code":400,"error":"'price' parameter missing in body"}

So we have to add the price value to the object:

{
"price" :0 }
response: 
HTTP/2 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
Content-Length: 17

{"price":"$0.00"}
so we updated the leadher jacket's price to 0. Refresh the site in the browser, and add the leather jacket to your cart for 0$

